WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.591
Hey there! Welcome to "Mathematical Experiments".

00:00:04.591 --> 00:00:10.960
Today, we'll be experimenting with approximating functions using polynomials.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.626
And, of course, we'll be learning Wolfram Mathematica by diving right in, just the way I like it.

00:00:16.626 --> 00:00:20.882
Without any extra explanations.

00:00:20.882 --> 00:00:26.139
So, today I want to talk to you about Chebyshev polynomials.

00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:37.004
You'll see how we can reach them on your own by solving a specific practical problem.

00:00:37.004 --> 00:00:43.434
Let's take a look at what these polynomials look like.

00:00:43.434 --> 00:00:47.035
Here's an example of one polynomial.

00:00:47.035 --> 00:00:49.812
And here's its plot.

00:00:51.579 --> 00:00:56.117
The higher the degree, the more oscillations within the interval (-1, 1).

00:00:56.117 --> 00:01:06.793
An important property is that the minima and maxima coincide in magnitude at -1 and 1.

00:01:06.793 --> 00:01:14.029
We'll get to these polynomials by solving the approximation problem using polynomials.

00:01:14.029 --> 00:01:22.602
We'll approximate this function f, which is called the Runge function.

00:01:22.602 --> 00:01:25.065
Here is its plot.

00:01:25.065 --> 00:01:29.524
There's a peak near zero.

00:01:30.780 --> 00:01:34.932
We'll try to approximate this function with a polynomial.

00:01:34.932 --> 00:01:38.530
Here's an example of a 12th-degree polynomial.

00:01:38.530 --> 00:01:44.173
The symbol "coeffs" contains the polynomial coefficients,

00:01:44.173 --> 00:01:49.554
and the symbol "poly" contains the polynomial itself.

00:01:49.554 --> 00:01:52.331
Let's do the next.

00:01:52.331 --> 00:01:54.048
Idea number 1:

00:01:54.048 --> 00:02:01.850
We'll take points on the interval (-1, 1), 13 of them.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:05.224
A uniform grid of points.

00:02:05.224 --> 00:02:07.059
Let's call it like this.

00:02:07.059 --> 00:02:09.561
And...

00:02:09.561 --> 00:02:17.127
we'll ask the polynomial to pass through these points exactly,

00:02:17.127 --> 00:02:21.600
taking the values that the function f takes at these points.

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:23.827
For example...

00:02:23.827 --> 00:02:26.722
An example of the second equation:

00:02:26.722 --> 00:02:31.737
the value of the polynomial at the point -0.833333

00:02:31.737 --> 00:02:35.299
is equal to this expression.

00:02:35.299 --> 00:02:43.415
And we equate this expression to the value of the function f at the point -0.833333

00:02:43.415 --> 00:02:49.058
We end up with 13 such equations for 13 coefficients.

00:02:49.058 --> 00:02:53.602
Mathematica easily finds the solution.

00:02:53.602 --> 00:02:57.329
Here's our polynomial.

00:02:57.329 --> 00:03:01.009
This polynomial, at the nodal points (let's call them that),

00:03:01.009 --> 00:03:09.604
passes through the same values as our function f.

00:03:09.604 --> 00:03:12.580
Our function f is the blue plot.

00:03:12.580 --> 00:03:15.632
And the result of the approximation is the yellow plot.

00:03:15.632 --> 00:03:27.517
Clearly, the yellow plot coincides with the function f at these 13 points.

00:03:27.517 --> 00:03:30.519
But we can't call this a good approximation.

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:32.865
If it's somewhat okay near zero,

00:03:32.865 --> 00:03:37.252
even there, we see differences between the blue and yellow plots.

00:03:37.252 --> 00:03:42.856
And towards the edges, it's a complete mess.

00:03:42.856 --> 00:03:49.245
If we take the difference, we'll see that the absolute difference is greater than one at the edges,

00:03:49.245 --> 00:03:52.541
while the function itself is smaller than one in magnitude.

00:03:52.541 --> 00:03:55.562
So, this is a bad approximation.

00:03:55.562 --> 00:03:59.752
If we try the Taylor series...

00:03:59.752 --> 00:04:02.725
Okay... better to take the 12th degree...

00:04:02.725 --> 00:04:06.232
...we still won't find anything good.

00:04:06.232 --> 00:04:12.472
In this plot, there are now

00:04:12.472 --> 00:04:14.150
three lines.

00:04:14.150 --> 00:04:16.229
The original function f is blue.

00:04:16.229 --> 00:04:19.752
Our approximation using the grid is yellow,

00:04:19.752 --> 00:04:22.833
and the Taylor series is green.

00:04:22.833 --> 00:04:30.724
The Taylor series precisely replicates our blue plot in the vicinity of zero,

00:04:30.724 --> 00:04:39.570
but beyond the interval (-0.2, 0.2), it's even worse than the yellow plot.

00:04:42.475 --> 00:04:47.058
So, two of our ideas turned out unsuccessful.

00:04:47.058 --> 00:04:52.976
Approximating the function using a grid of points is a bad idea.

00:04:52.976 --> 00:04:58.579
Using the Taylor series doesn't give a good solution either,

00:04:58.579 --> 00:05:03.829
if we need accuracy over the entire interval (-1, 1).

00:05:03.829 --> 00:05:06.831
We need some third idea.

00:05:06.831 --> 00:05:17.263
Our Idea #1, about the grid, might be successful if we arrange the nodal points in another way.

00:05:17.263 --> 00:05:20.226
For example,

00:05:20.226 --> 00:05:24.439
let's place them closer to the edges of the interval (-1, 1).

00:05:24.439 --> 00:05:32.854
The problems arise precisely at the edges of the interval, and if we place more nodal points there,

00:05:32.854 --> 00:05:37.947
where our function matches the original one,

00:05:37.947 --> 00:05:42.648
we might overcome these problems.

00:05:42.648 --> 00:05:48.465
Let me explain why the uniform grid from -1 to 1 is bad,

00:05:48.465 --> 00:05:50.261
using this polynomial as an example.

00:05:50.261 --> 00:05:59.720
Let's plot the polynomial that takes the value 0 at all nodal points.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:05.853
This polynomial has slight oscillations around zero,

00:06:05.853 --> 00:06:11.692
but the closer we get to the edges of the interval (-1, 1), the larger the oscillations become.

00:06:11.692 --> 00:06:15.136
That's it's problem.

00:06:15.136 --> 00:06:17.136
Here's an idea:

00:06:17.136 --> 00:06:27.333
Let's find a grid where the oscillations throughout the interval have the same amplitude.

00:06:27.333 --> 00:06:30.385
That's roughly what this idea sounds like.

00:06:30.385 --> 00:06:34.759
Next, I try to explain something complex here.

00:06:34.759 --> 00:06:38.125
I take this polynomial

00:06:38.125 --> 00:06:41.538
and remove the i-th factor from it.

00:06:41.538 --> 00:06:49.458
And I draw various polynomials, 13 of them,

00:06:49.458 --> 00:06:58.712
to show this problem that if we remove the i-th factor even from the polynomial, then at the i-th node...

00:06:58.712 --> 00:07:02.405
(in this case, I removed the factor x - 0)

00:07:02.405 --> 00:07:10.000
...the values modulo are smaller than some intervals between nodes.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:11.372
That's bad.

00:07:11.372 --> 00:07:14.816
Later, you'll understand why.

00:07:14.816 --> 00:07:17.986
Now I won't specify.

00:07:17.986 --> 00:07:26.125
Here, I want to draw all our basis functions.

00:07:26.125 --> 00:07:29.491
Here's how it looks.

00:07:29.491 --> 00:07:32.464
All 13 basis functions.

00:07:32.464 --> 00:07:35.817
Or, more precisely, here it's 11.

00:07:35.817 --> 00:07:39.760
Here are the actual 13 basis functions.

00:07:42.312 --> 00:07:46.396
The problem with these basis functions is that,

00:07:46.396 --> 00:07:54.010
when we look at a specific basis function and the node that was removed from it,

00:07:54.010 --> 00:08:05.816
at this node, it takes values modulo smaller than in some intervals between others.

00:08:05.816 --> 00:08:07.729
That's one of the problems.

00:08:07.729 --> 00:08:10.781
Maybe it's a difficult and incomprehensible explanation,

00:08:10.781 --> 00:08:20.196
but let's skip it for now and move on to this idea that was mentioned.

00:08:20.196 --> 00:08:27.684
To find a polynomial that oscillates equally over the interval (-1, 1).

00:08:27.684 --> 00:08:33.644
That is, all its maxima and minima have the same value modulo.

00:08:33.644 --> 00:08:43.380
At the end of the video, I attempted to explain the idea of proving the theorem that the polynomial, which is the solution to the minmax problem, must be equioscillating, meaning the values modulo of its extreme values should coincide.

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:49.607
This can be written as the following code.

00:08:49.607 --> 00:08:57.220
Let's describe the polynomial as a product of factors x - r[i].

00:08:57.220 --> 00:08:59.178
Table generates the list of factors.

00:08:59.178 --> 00:09:03.094
Times multiplies all these factors.

00:09:03.094 --> 00:09:07.991
And we'll ask for the maximum

00:09:07.991 --> 00:09:15.204
and the values of this polynomial on a grid of points from -1 to 1

00:09:15.204 --> 00:09:20.000
to have the maximum of all these values as small as possible.

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:20.926
So, now it goes like this:

00:09:20.926 --> 00:09:31.398
Minimize the maximum value of the polynomial on the interval (-1, 1).

00:09:31.398 --> 00:09:33.154
Running...

00:09:33.154 --> 00:09:36.402
It's thinking for a while.

00:09:36.402 --> 00:09:39.128
Let's see...

00:09:39.128 --> 00:09:41.669
Here's the result.

00:09:41.669 --> 00:09:45.192
Let's draw this polynomial.

00:09:45.192 --> 00:09:47.303
Here it is.

00:09:47.303 --> 00:09:49.112
See?

00:09:49.112 --> 00:09:58.916
We asked the polynomial to have the smallest value modulo on the interval (-1, 1),

00:09:58.916 --> 00:10:05.099
and it produced a polynomial called "Equioscillating".

00:10:05.099 --> 00:10:13.713
So, these are the modules of the extreme points.

00:10:13.713 --> 00:10:15.770
Well... In this case, they are approximately equal.

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:19.568
As you can see, for example, this maximum is slightly larger than this one.

00:10:19.568 --> 00:10:26.114
And this actually indicates that Mathematica found not the absolute optimum.

00:10:26.114 --> 00:10:31.991
It's not the best solution to this problem.

00:10:31.991 --> 00:10:40.000
Anyway, let's draw how the roots of this polynomial look like.

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.638
Let's draw the list of roots.

00:10:41.638 --> 00:10:44.376
The X-axis here represents the i-th number,

00:10:44.376 --> 00:10:48.409
and the Y-axis represents the value of the root.

00:10:48.409 --> 00:10:54.601
You can see that the roots are denser near the edges,

00:10:54.601 --> 00:10:57.698
then the distance between the roots increases,

00:10:57.698 --> 00:11:00.789
and then it becomes denser again.

00:11:02.948 --> 00:11:09.598
Some mathematician might say, "Oh! This looks very similar to a sinusoid!"

00:11:09.598 --> 00:11:16.458
More precisely, to the function -cos[αx].

00:11:16.458 --> 00:11:18.144
Let's test this idea.

00:11:18.144 --> 00:11:22.570
To do that, we need to take the arcsine or arccosine

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:25.111
of these values of the roots

00:11:25.111 --> 00:11:29.773
and see

00:11:29.773 --> 00:11:35.180
if the gotten points lie on a straight line.

00:11:38.789 --> 00:11:45.342
Here I drew the best-fitting line through these points

00:11:45.342 --> 00:11:52.084
and found that they almost perfectly lie on a straight line.

00:11:52.084 --> 00:11:57.582
This is the first way to approach Chebyshev polynomials

00:11:57.582 --> 00:12:01.890
and understand how they work.

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:08.985
Although I believe that Pafnuty Chebyshev himself took a different path,

00:12:08.985 --> 00:12:11.397
which I will explain later.

00:12:11.397 --> 00:12:12.545
So, the idea is:

00:12:12.545 --> 00:12:21.285
the nodes for which we must set the equality for our polynomial and the original function f,

00:12:21.285 --> 00:12:33.294
form a cosine of some arithmetic progression on the interval (-1, 1).

00:12:33.294 --> 00:12:36.820
More precisely, on the interval from 0 to Pi.

00:12:36.820 --> 00:12:44.048
You can see that the points are located from 0 to Pi, but do not reach Pi

00:12:44.048 --> 00:12:47.461
and do not reach zero.

00:12:47.461 --> 00:12:54.771
But here, I deliberately chose the wrong variant and placed the extreme points right at zero and right at Pi.

00:12:58.029 --> 00:13:05.807
Thus, I got cos[1] and cos[-1].

00:13:05.807 --> 00:13:08.571
Let's visualize this.

00:13:08.571 --> 00:13:21.698
On the upper half-circle, I placed 13 evenly distributed points and projected them onto the X-axis.

00:13:21.698 --> 00:13:27.311
Then I wrote the product of the terms x - x[i].

00:13:27.311 --> 00:13:30.245
And I failed.

00:13:30.245 --> 00:13:35.220
It turned out to be non-equioscillating.

00:13:35.220 --> 00:13:38.524
Because I intentionally made a mistake.

00:13:38.524 --> 00:13:44.845
Since Mathematica told us not to reach 0 and not to reach Pi.

00:13:44.845 --> 00:13:53.384
So, let's listen to it this time and make thе next change.

00:13:53.384 --> 00:13:56.162
We will divide by n + 1 instead of n,

00:13:56.162 --> 00:14:00.990
and make a shift by 0.5 here.

00:14:00.990 --> 00:14:02.597
Here we go...

00:14:02.597 --> 00:14:08.763
Now we get an equioscillating polynomial.

00:14:08.763 --> 00:14:10.475
Visualization.

00:14:10.475 --> 00:14:13.919
You see? There are no points on the circle.

00:14:13.919 --> 00:14:16.193
The first point goes through half the interval,

00:14:16.193 --> 00:14:18.970
then: interval, interval, interval, and so on,

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:21.677
and then again half the interval,

00:14:21.677 --> 00:14:24.698
and here there are no extreme points at all.

00:14:24.698 --> 00:14:33.128
These are the roots of Chebyshev polynomials.

00:14:33.128 --> 00:14:40.263
Let's see how the approximation based on these nodes looks like.

00:14:40.263 --> 00:14:45.932
Now for some reason, odd powers start to appear.

00:14:45.932 --> 00:14:50.157
By the way, they can be removed with a simple replacement rule.

00:14:50.157 --> 00:14:53.641
Let's draw...

00:14:53.641 --> 00:15:00.344
and you can see that the green approximation is much better than the yellow one we had.

00:15:00.344 --> 00:15:02.179
Alright.

00:15:04.691 --> 00:15:08.823
So, here are our wonderful nodes.

00:15:08.823 --> 00:15:15.486
They are called Chebyshev nodes.

00:15:15.486 --> 00:15:23.444
And again, we can consider a set of basis functions that are constructed as follows:

00:15:23.444 --> 00:15:28.105
we take the product of all the terms x - x[i],

00:15:28.105 --> 00:15:32.048
but exclude one of these terms.

00:15:32.048 --> 00:15:36.199
Here, for example, a term with a node located somewhere here is excluded.

00:15:36.199 --> 00:15:38.858
And here, we already see a remarkable property.

00:15:38.858 --> 00:15:43.139
The value of this basis function modulo at the node is much larger

00:15:43.139 --> 00:15:49.410
than the value of this basis function at all other points of the interval.

00:15:49.410 --> 00:15:53.404
And this holds true for any i.

00:15:53.404 --> 00:15:54.611
Okay...

00:15:54.611 --> 00:15:59.547
Let's assume you understood this idea.

00:15:59.547 --> 00:16:06.407
Here are all the basis functions for n = 30 depicted.

00:16:10.372 --> 00:16:13.828
And here I can even explain.

00:16:13.828 --> 00:16:26.184
Each of these bumps corresponds to the value of the basis function at the node that was excluded from it.

00:16:27.519 --> 00:16:32.561
And, for example, this second-level bump,

00:16:32.561 --> 00:16:39.500
it is adjacent to the node that was excluded.

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:54.407
You can see that with increasing n, the ratio of the maximum at the excluded node

00:16:54.407 --> 00:17:00.508
to the value of the basis function at other points increases as n grows.

00:17:00.508 --> 00:17:04.087
That is...

00:17:04.087 --> 00:17:11.073
the approximation becomes better and better the more points you take.

00:17:11.073 --> 00:17:17.981
And when you try to approximate the function f using Chebyshev nodes,

00:17:17.981 --> 00:17:22.873
you are, to some approximation, solving this problem:

00:17:22.873 --> 00:17:34.090
"Find a polynomial for which the maximum modulus deviation from the original function over the interval (-1, 1) is minimal".

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:41.481
But Chebyshev nodes do not give an exact solution to this problem.

00:17:41.481 --> 00:17:43.355
Next, what I wanted to show here...

00:17:43.355 --> 00:17:49.006
Ah! I also wanted to explain another example with this idea of a basis.

00:17:49.006 --> 00:17:51.561
Look, here I took the fifth basis function,

00:17:51.561 --> 00:17:53.396
the eighth one.

00:17:53.396 --> 00:17:56.683
I multiplied the first one by three... it doesn't matter by what.

00:17:56.683 --> 00:17:58.801
And the second one by -7.

00:17:58.801 --> 00:18:02.834
And got these modified basis functions.

00:18:02.834 --> 00:18:05.061
The yellow one,

00:18:05.061 --> 00:18:08.112
it's exactly 3b[5],

00:18:08.112 --> 00:18:13.598
and the green one is -7b[8].

00:18:13.598 --> 00:18:21.282
And the function f2 is the sum of these two basis functions, multiplied by some coefficients.

00:18:21.282 --> 00:18:30.977
And you see? The green function passes through the same point in this node where the yellow one passes through,

00:18:30.977 --> 00:18:38.151
and in this node, it passes through the same point as the green one.

00:18:38.151 --> 00:18:43.505
So, you kind of multiply each basis polynomial by the number

00:18:43.505 --> 00:18:51.433
to get the desired value at the node that was excluded from it.

00:18:51.433 --> 00:18:56.110
And when you then sum up all the basis functions with these coefficients,

00:18:56.110 --> 00:18:58.544
you get a function with the desired properties.

00:18:58.544 --> 00:19:12.352
It will pass through all the nodes at the exact same y-values that the function f takes.

00:19:12.352 --> 00:19:16.512
Okay... I probably better skip ahead...

00:19:16.512 --> 00:19:27.180
Here, I will explain how to get Chebyshev polynomials in Mathematica.

00:19:27.180 --> 00:19:31.292
There's already a ready-made function ChebyshevT.

00:19:31.292 --> 00:19:35.482
There are also Chebyshev polynomials of the second kind, ChebyshevU.

00:19:35.482 --> 00:19:46.464
They are simply the derivatives of ChebyshevT polynomials with respect to x and some other factor 1/n.

00:19:46.464 --> 00:19:49.072
The plot looks like this.

00:19:49.072 --> 00:19:53.696
Well... you already understood how it all works.

00:19:53.696 --> 00:19:58.161
Here, several Chebyshev polynomials are already plotted together.

00:19:58.161 --> 00:20:02.002
We can add the first one.

00:20:02.002 --> 00:20:03.832
And one more thing.

00:20:03.832 --> 00:20:16.167
How could you quickly get polynomials that oscillate between the values (-1, 1)?

00:20:16.167 --> 00:20:19.805
Take the polynomial t2 = 2x^2 - 1.

00:20:19.805 --> 00:20:23.642
It possesses this remarkable property.

00:20:23.642 --> 00:20:30.702
Over the interval (-1, 1), it takes values from -1 to 1.

00:20:30.702 --> 00:20:35.913
From 1, it goes to -1, then back to 1.

00:20:35.913 --> 00:20:38.577
And...

00:20:38.577 --> 00:20:43.670
You can substitute this polynomial into itself

00:20:43.670 --> 00:20:52.807
and get increasingly intricate functions with a greater number of extrema over the interval (-1, 1),

00:20:52.807 --> 00:20:56.605
and all of them will be the same in absolute value.

00:21:02.376 --> 00:21:04.121
So...

00:21:04.121 --> 00:21:14.749
However, this method allows you to generate Equioscillating polynomials with degrees of 2^k.

00:21:14.749 --> 00:21:18.810
2, 4, 8, and so on.

00:21:18.810 --> 00:21:23.550
But it turns out that there are also all the intermediate values of n.

00:21:23.550 --> 00:21:25.503
Like these.

00:21:25.503 --> 00:21:30.251
And they are called Chebyshev polynomials.

00:21:33.863 --> 00:21:39.823
Here, I have drawn the Chebyshev polynomials of the second kind.

00:21:43.199 --> 00:21:48.790
How do I think Chebyshev came up with them.

00:21:48.790 --> 00:21:57.970
Of course, he couldn't just call the NMinimize function.

00:21:57.970 --> 00:22:02.071
He didn't have such an opportunity.

00:22:02.071 --> 00:22:05.524
So, when he called NMinimize,

00:22:05.524 --> 00:22:07.202
looked at this polynomial,

00:22:07.202 --> 00:22:09.037
looked at this plot,

00:22:09.037 --> 00:22:10.872
and then took the arccosine,

00:22:10.872 --> 00:22:12.999
that's just my imagination, of course.

00:22:12.999 --> 00:22:14.952
Most likely, it wasn't like that.

00:22:14.952 --> 00:22:21.380
It's not that simple to solve this minimization problem on paper.

00:22:21.380 --> 00:22:25.452
Most likely, another method was used.

00:22:25.452 --> 00:22:27.326
My favorite method.

00:22:27.326 --> 00:22:35.380
When you just engage in random wandering, so to speak.

00:22:35.380 --> 00:22:42.130
Or rather, you develop your thoughts in various interesting directions for you,

00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:45.927
accumulate a base of mathematical facts.

00:22:45.927 --> 00:22:52.276
And then, when you encounter a practical problem, an enlightenment suddenly comes,

00:22:52.276 --> 00:22:57.013
because you have accumulated a base of facts.

00:22:57.013 --> 00:23:00.000
Most likely, such a method worked for Chebyshev,

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:11.709
and he probably knew because he had accumulated such an interesting fact from life that

00:23:11.709 --> 00:23:20.746
Cos[nx] can be represented as a polynomial of Cos[x].

00:23:20.746 --> 00:23:22.333
Just a moment...

00:23:22.333 --> 00:23:24.364
Let me explain it.

00:23:24.364 --> 00:23:25.257
Look...

00:23:25.257 --> 00:23:33.718
Suppose you know these two expansion formulas for Cos[a + b] and Sin[a + b].

00:23:33.718 --> 00:23:42.980
From these formulas, it immediately follows that you can transform Cos[nx] into a polynomial of Cos[x] and Sin[x].

00:23:42.980 --> 00:23:51.921
But, oh, miracle! When you do this, you find that Sin[x] appears with even powers,

00:23:51.921 --> 00:23:55.719
which means you can apply this formula

00:23:55.719 --> 00:24:02.415
and eventually write this polynomial exclusively in terms of Cos[x].

00:24:02.415 --> 00:24:11.120
I believe this mathematical fact from trigonometry was known to mathematicians 200 years ago

00:24:11.120 --> 00:24:14.162
and an enlightenment occurred.

00:24:14.162 --> 00:24:25.812
Cos[nx], in a sense, by definition - it's a number from -1 to 1.

00:24:25.812 --> 00:24:32.955
And therefore, Cos[nx] as a polynomial of Cos[x] is exactly what we need.

00:24:32.955 --> 00:24:38.166
By the way, it will indeed take values (-1, 1) multiple times,

00:24:38.166 --> 00:24:41.579
the larger n, the more frequently.

00:24:41.579 --> 00:24:45.377
Okay... Now I'll explain this on another example.

00:24:45.377 --> 00:24:48.184
Let's take this Cos[5x],

00:24:48.184 --> 00:24:54.345
then substitute -Sin[x]^n into this expression.

00:24:57.250 --> 00:24:58.601
In short...

00:24:58.601 --> 00:25:06.921
Cos[5x] as a polynomial of Cos[x] is exactly the Chebyshev polynomial.

00:25:06.921 --> 00:25:10.163
What else...

00:25:14.089 --> 00:25:20.566
How to arrive at the recurrence formula for Chebyshev polynomials?

00:25:20.566 --> 00:25:24.442
This is done as follows:

00:25:24.442 --> 00:25:29.653
Perhaps someone knows the formula Cos[a + b] + Cos[a - b],

00:25:29.653 --> 00:25:34.323
but those, who don't, can get it from these two formulas.

00:25:34.323 --> 00:25:39.141
We simply add the first and second equalities

00:25:39.141 --> 00:25:42.389
and get the following expression.

00:25:42.389 --> 00:25:50.762
And from it, it directly follows that

00:25:50.762 --> 00:25:59.843
Chebyshev polynomial [n + 1] plus Chebyshev polynomial [n - 1]

00:25:59.843 --> 00:26:06.250
equals 2 times Chebyshev polynomial of n multiplied by x.

00:26:06.250 --> 00:26:10.000
Well, or it can be written like this.

00:26:10.000 --> 00:26:15.043
The first two values are as follows.

00:26:15.043 --> 00:26:20.000
And from this definition, from these three lines,

00:26:20.000 --> 00:26:27.208
it becomes quite clear that these are indeed polynomials, not some other functions.

00:26:27.208 --> 00:26:35.283
In addition, from this recurrence formula, you can get the generating function for Chebyshev polynomials.

00:26:35.283 --> 00:26:37.628
Here's how it's defined.

00:26:37.628 --> 00:26:41.354
You need to do this once, and then you will understand

00:26:41.354 --> 00:26:52.914
how to get the generating function from the recurrence formula.

00:26:52.914 --> 00:26:57.369
We take, according to this recurrence formula,

00:26:57.369 --> 00:27:01.763
the generating function of x, t,

00:27:01.763 --> 00:27:06.472
minus 2tx,

00:27:08.592 --> 00:27:13.487
and plus (t^2) G[x, t].

00:27:13.487 --> 00:27:16.743
Let's calculate this expression.

00:27:16.743 --> 00:27:18.133
Here's what it equals.

00:27:18.133 --> 00:27:21.182
Here I just rewrote it.

00:27:21.182 --> 00:27:24.509
Next, we put t here,

00:27:24.509 --> 00:27:27.407
and we get t^n+1.

00:27:27.407 --> 00:27:29.680
We put this t^2 here.

00:27:29.680 --> 00:27:33.290
We get t^n+2.

00:27:33.290 --> 00:27:37.088
Next, we perform a renumbering.

00:27:37.088 --> 00:27:39.590
Here we sum from 1

00:27:39.590 --> 00:27:44.212
and no longer write n + 1 here, but just n.

00:27:47.039 --> 00:27:50.520
And here we write, accordingly, n - 1.

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:54.828
And here we write t^n, but sum from 2,

00:27:54.828 --> 00:27:58.437
and here n - 2.

00:27:58.437 --> 00:28:07.770
Next, we combine these three sums into one sum and take t^n out of the brackets.

00:28:10.754 --> 00:28:17.565
However, from the first sum, only the first two terms remain,

00:28:17.565 --> 00:28:19.332
here they are,

00:28:19.332 --> 00:28:22.117
and from the second sum, only one term remains,

00:28:22.117 --> 00:28:23.960
here it is.

00:28:25.844 --> 00:28:30.000
This sum is equal to zero simply because

00:28:30.000 --> 00:28:32.109
this expression is equal to zero.

00:28:32.109 --> 00:28:37.987
This is the same recurrence formula, just written

00:28:37.987 --> 00:28:40.764
like this.

00:28:40.764 --> 00:28:45.324
With a minus sign and it still equals zero.

00:28:47.836 --> 00:28:53.283
Therefore, what we originally calculated,

00:28:53.283 --> 00:28:56.374
is equal to just this expression.

00:28:56.374 --> 00:28:58.385
That is, this

00:28:58.385 --> 00:29:00.425
equals this.

00:29:00.425 --> 00:29:02.613
So, we have got the answer.

00:29:02.613 --> 00:29:07.903
Let's check that everything is fine.

00:29:07.903 --> 00:29:09.110
Here we go...

00:29:10.288 --> 00:29:14.280
In this way, Chebyshev polynomials are gotten.

00:29:14.280 --> 00:29:23.221
With t^n, it turns out to be the Chebyshev polynomial of degree n.

00:29:23.221 --> 00:29:30.000
And this polynomial is a solution to the minmax problem

00:29:30.000 --> 00:29:32.463
with an accuracy up to the coefficient.

00:29:32.463 --> 00:29:36.496
It is a solution to this problem.

00:29:36.496 --> 00:29:38.095
Okay...

00:29:39.037 --> 00:29:44.413
Today, I also want to remind you about Bernoulli polynomials and draw them.

00:29:44.413 --> 00:29:50.566
The thing is that they, in a sense, are also equioscillating.

00:29:50.566 --> 00:29:54.874
For example, the 15th Bernoulli polynomial.

00:29:56.641 --> 00:29:58.251
See?

00:29:58.251 --> 00:30:02.480
Here the nodes are evenly spaced,

00:30:02.480 --> 00:30:07.612
but the number of oscillations is small, less than 15.

00:30:09.339 --> 00:30:12.329
Here is the 16th Bernoulli polynomial,

00:30:12.329 --> 00:30:14.870
and you can see that it's not quite Equioscillating.

00:30:14.870 --> 00:30:16.868
Look at this extremum

00:30:16.868 --> 00:30:21.891
and this one, they're kind of strange, extreme points, they have different values.

00:30:21.891 --> 00:30:25.985
Here's the 17th.

00:30:25.985 --> 00:30:29.123
Here's the 18th.

00:30:29.123 --> 00:30:30.196
Look how interesting.

00:30:30.196 --> 00:30:34.268
It's as if the Bernoulli polynomials try to fit the sine wave,

00:30:34.268 --> 00:30:40.892
but at some point, they deviate and go into a power dependence.

00:30:40.892 --> 00:30:43.551
That's really the case.

00:30:43.551 --> 00:30:50.411
Indeed, the Bernoulli polynomials, in a sense, try to approximate the sine wave

00:30:50.411 --> 00:30:52.285
as best as possible,

00:30:52.285 --> 00:30:58.006
and with each number n, they approximate this sine wave better and better,

00:30:58.006 --> 00:31:02.833
and over a larger interval.

00:31:02.833 --> 00:31:05.257
Let's draw

00:31:05.257 --> 00:31:08.858
here several Bernoulli polynomials,

00:31:08.858 --> 00:31:10.928
only we'll cleverly normalize them...

00:31:10.928 --> 00:31:15.668
Don't pay attention, this normalization doesn't have any deep meaning.

00:31:15.668 --> 00:31:19.565
...and let's also multiply them by 1 and -1 to see

00:31:19.565 --> 00:31:24.619
these two families explicitly.

00:31:24.619 --> 00:31:28.801
Even and odd Bernoulli polynomials.

00:31:28.801 --> 00:31:34.640
And you can see that the lower-degree one (green here) deviates earlier,

00:31:34.640 --> 00:31:45.975
while the higher the degree, the later the deviation to something different from a sine wave.

00:31:45.975 --> 00:31:54.287
The higher the polynomial number, the farther it goes along the sine wave to the left and right.

00:31:56.760 --> 00:32:01.756
Alright... what else I want to show here...

00:32:04.975 --> 00:32:07.150
Example of a 25th-degree polynomial.

00:32:07.150 --> 00:32:20.000
Without normalization, its extrema oscillate between (-350000, 350000).

00:32:20.000 --> 00:32:25.926
That is, the magnitude of the oscillations increases.

00:32:25.926 --> 00:32:30.866
But what I want to draw attention to.

00:32:30.866 --> 00:32:37.294
Let's take the 15th-degree polynomial

00:32:37.294 --> 00:32:44.885
and see where the roots of the derivative

00:32:44.885 --> 00:32:45.867
of this polynomial are.

00:32:45.867 --> 00:32:52.334
In other words, find the x-values for extreme points.

00:32:52.334 --> 00:32:54.625
Here are their values.

00:32:54.625 --> 00:33:00.852
-1.22213, 0.74999

00:33:00.852 --> 00:33:04.846
Let's calculate the intervals between these roots.

00:33:04.846 --> 00:33:13.512
When you have a list of numbers and want to calculate the differences between neighboring elements, use ListConvolve.

00:33:13.512 --> 00:33:18.605
The difference between this root and this one is 0.472...

00:33:18.605 --> 00:33:25.074
Between this one and this one, it's something very close to 0.5

00:33:25.074 --> 00:33:26.084
There you go...

00:33:26.084 --> 00:33:34.480
You can see that the extrema are not strictly spaced at 0.5

00:33:39.819 --> 00:33:47.409
And the values of the extrema themselves are also not perfectly equal modulo.

00:33:47.409 --> 00:33:49.409
See?

00:33:50.665 --> 00:33:58.940
Even between this value and this one, there are differences in some distant signs.

00:34:02.589 --> 00:34:10.600
This can be shown by plotting a small piece of the plane x/y.

00:34:10.600 --> 00:34:12.199
So...

00:34:14.358 --> 00:34:16.692
Yes, here's another interesting picture.

00:34:16.692 --> 00:34:22.413
I draw the Bernoulli polynomial in blue,

00:34:22.413 --> 00:34:28.291
and on top of it, I'm plotting the cosine function.

00:34:28.291 --> 00:34:30.000
Look...

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:31.058
See?

00:34:31.058 --> 00:34:35.209
The Bernoulli polynomials try to be equal to the cosine,

00:34:35.209 --> 00:34:39.007
but at some point, they deviate and go to infinity.

00:34:39.007 --> 00:34:43.904
This is exclusively for even numbers.

00:34:43.904 --> 00:34:49.586
A very interesting property of the Bernoulli polynomials that I hadn't heard of.

00:34:49.586 --> 00:34:55.229
I wonder if any of you know about this property.

00:34:55.229 --> 00:34:58.673
Has it been studied anywhere?

00:34:58.673 --> 00:35:06.282
The difference between the Chebyshev polynomial and the cosine is quite small.

00:35:06.282 --> 00:35:12.317
See? 10^-7 for the 24th-degree polynomial.

00:35:15.654 --> 00:35:20.245
And this difference decreases significantly with the growth of n.

00:35:20.245 --> 00:35:23.886
Such an amazing property.

00:35:23.886 --> 00:35:28.233
The Bernoulli polynomials are almost Equioscillating,

00:35:28.233 --> 00:35:33.962
and if you want to approximate a function on a uniform grid,

00:35:33.962 --> 00:35:36.175
then you should use Bernoulli polynomials,

00:35:36.175 --> 00:35:43.977
but, of course, you'll have to take higher degrees than the number of points you have.

00:35:47.667 --> 00:35:49.713
Here, I've drawn a cool picture too.

00:35:49.713 --> 00:35:57.562
These are the roots of the Bernoulli polynomial on the complex plane.

00:36:01.959 --> 00:36:10.454
I've already mentioned, that the Bernoulli polynomials have fewer oscillations

00:36:10.454 --> 00:36:12.650
than their degree.

00:36:12.650 --> 00:36:17.665
Why? Because some of their roots

00:36:17.665 --> 00:36:21.345
have non-zero imaginary parts.

00:36:21.345 --> 00:36:24.819
And here's how these roots are distributed.

00:36:24.819 --> 00:36:29.441
That is the idea that you need to place more roots at the edge.

00:36:29.441 --> 00:36:31.598
The Bernoulli polynomials are realized as follows:

00:36:31.598 --> 00:36:38.461
they placed them at the edge, but in the complex plane,

00:36:38.461 --> 00:36:49.530
and in the middle, they still made Equidistance, approximately the same distance between the roots.

00:36:52.985 --> 00:36:58.075
And if you remove one of these roots,

00:36:58.075 --> 00:37:04.307
you'll get this wonderful property again, that

00:37:04.307 --> 00:37:09.479
at this point

00:37:09.479 --> 00:37:16.731
the value of the Bernoulli polynomial is greater than anywhere else on this interval.

00:37:16.731 --> 00:37:21.370
But there's a certain unpleasantness here.

00:37:21.370 --> 00:37:26.580
There is no reduction of the ratio of the modulus value at this point

00:37:26.580 --> 00:37:33.636
and the modulus at all other points on the interval.

00:37:33.636 --> 00:37:37.206
And also, this unusual elongation of the interval itself.

00:37:37.206 --> 00:37:39.316
It's not the interval (-1, 1),

00:37:39.316 --> 00:37:44.841
but a gradually increasing interval.

00:37:44.841 --> 00:37:48.325
And one more thing I forgot to mention.

00:37:48.325 --> 00:37:58.326
The fact that there is a problem of finding a polynomial for which the maximum value of the modulus is minimal on the interval.

00:37:58.326 --> 00:38:09.818
And there's a problem of making a polynomial that has the same extreme points.

00:38:09.818 --> 00:38:13.357
And it turns out

00:38:13.357 --> 00:38:21.011
this extreme polynomial has the Equioscillation property.

00:38:21.011 --> 00:38:25.240
How to prove this theorem? Equioscillation theorem.

00:38:25.240 --> 00:38:29.132
I don't claim to have a completely rigorous proof.

00:38:29.132 --> 00:38:32.812
The idea is as follows... by contradiction:

00:38:32.812 --> 00:38:38.781
let's assume we have a polynomial that is a solution to this problem.

00:38:38.781 --> 00:38:42.893
And let's assume it's not Equioscillating.

00:38:42.893 --> 00:38:49.232
Let's say it has an extreme point whose magnitude is greater than all the others, for example.

00:38:49.232 --> 00:38:52.367
If there are several, that's a complex case. It needs to be considered separately.

00:38:52.367 --> 00:38:57.895
But let's say there's one outstanding extreme point, a maximum,

00:38:57.895 --> 00:39:01.585
that is larger in magnitude than all other maxima.

00:39:01.585 --> 00:39:05.594
Then we can do the following:

00:39:05.594 --> 00:39:11.264
let's take, say, a 13th-degree polynomial and we have 13 coefficients.

00:39:11.264 --> 00:39:14.394
More precisely, 12, because it's a monic polynomial.

00:39:14.394 --> 00:39:20.000
And let's consider this 12-dimensional space of coefficients.

00:39:20.000 --> 00:39:25.368
And let's consider a small neighborhood around the current point,

00:39:25.368 --> 00:39:31.843
around the values of these coefficients gotten by solving this problem.

00:39:31.843 --> 00:39:38.707
And let's take such a small vicinity, a 12-dimensional sphere, its interior,

00:39:38.707 --> 00:39:41.812
so that within this sphere, this property is not violated,

00:39:41.812 --> 00:39:46.285
and our max maximum is still greater than all other maxima.

00:39:46.285 --> 00:39:51.456
Let's take such a small sphere that this property holds.

00:39:51.456 --> 00:39:52.859
Next.

00:39:52.859 --> 00:40:00.440
Note that at our extreme node,

00:40:00.440 --> 00:40:06.484
the values of the polynomial are just a linear function of the coefficients.

00:40:06.484 --> 00:40:09.536
Of these 12 coefficients.

00:40:09.536 --> 00:40:13.530
This means that in our 12-dimensional sphere, there is a direction

00:40:13.530 --> 00:40:20.625
in which we can reduce the value of this maximum.

00:40:20.625 --> 00:40:26.228
Let's move to this point on the sphere,

00:40:26.228 --> 00:40:30.898
where the maximum is reduced.

00:40:30.898 --> 00:40:42.665
Then we get an answer that better solves the problem of minimizing the maximum.

00:40:42.665 --> 00:40:44.415
And we get a contradiction.

00:40:44.415 --> 00:40:50.157
So, our original polynomial is apparently not a solution to our problem,

00:40:50.157 --> 00:40:53.945
and we assumed it was.

00:40:53.945 --> 00:40:57.570
Therefore, for polynomials,

00:40:57.570 --> 00:41:01.454
which are solutions to this problem,

00:41:01.454 --> 00:41:09.657
there cannot be a situation where the extremes by modulus have different values.

00:41:09.657 --> 00:41:13.101
This is not a rigorous proof.

00:41:13.101 --> 00:41:17.224
Its lack of rigor is due to the fact that

00:41:17.224 --> 00:41:20.884
while I managed to reduce the value of the maximum at the node,

00:41:20.884 --> 00:41:26.771
there's no guarantee that a little to the right, a new maximum won't appear, even larger.

00:41:26.771 --> 00:41:38.224
And I didn't consider the case where there are several maximums of the same magnitude.

00:41:38.224 --> 00:41:41.369
But these cases can be carefully considered.

00:41:41.369 --> 00:41:46.737
It's better to just read Wikipedia and find out how this theorem is proved.

00:41:46.737 --> 00:41:50.574
That's all for today. Bye, everyone!

